.TH gromox\-mbck 8 "" "Gromox" "Gromox admin reference"
.SH Name
\fBgromox\-mbck\fP \(em Mailbox check and repair utility
.SH Synopsis
\fBgromox\-mbck\fP [\fB\-p\fP] exchange.sqlite3 [...]
.SH Description
mbck checks one or more mailbox databases (exmdb/exchange.sqlite3) for
specific inconsistencies in their internal bookkeeping. Use it when
investigating suspected mailbox database damage, for example after a database
recovery, or when directed to do so by support.
.PP
The main check verifies that every positive folder and message ID is covered
by an entry in the allocated_eids table. Missing entries mean the database
does not record those IDs as allocated. With \fB\-p\fP, mbck inserts allocation
records for the affected IDs. Without \fB\-p\fP, it reports the affected IDs
and issue counts without applying repairs.
.PP
An additional index-related diagnostic is present, but does not establish
that all required indexes exist; index repair is not implemented. mbck is
not a general SQLite integrity checker and does not verify message bodies or
attachments in the cid directory.
.PP
Back up the mailbox before using \fB\-p\fP.
.PP
mbck directly operates on the filesystem, which is not ideal, but it is
believed it is "mostly fine":
.PP
It is technically safe to run gromox\-mbck while gromox\-http has a mailbox
open, provided Gromox is version >= 2.30. HOWEVER, gromox\-http (still as of
Gromox 2.36) does not anticipate databases being write-locked by another
process for undue amounts of time (gromox.cfg:sqlite_busy_timeout), and signals
an operational error to the caller. For example, mail cannot be delivered to
the mailbox while mbck is running in repair/write mode.
.SH Options
.TP
\fB\-p\fP
Perform repairs / write operations. (Default: just readonly checks)
.TP
\fB\-?\fP
Display option summary.
